Introduction
Every explainability technique applied to a trading model after the fact makes a promise it can't quite keep. SHAP values approximate what a black-box network is doing by perturbing inputs and averaging the effect. Partial dependence plots average over a background dataset and hope the interactions wash out. Both are useful, and both are guesses about a model whose real internal computation nobody can point to directly.
A Neural Additive Model (NAM) sidesteps the guessing entirely. Instead of one dense network where every input is tangled with every other input by the second layer, a NAM assigns each feature its own small subnetwork, and the final prediction is nothing more than the sum of what those subnetworks output. There is no approximation step because there is nothing to approximate — the contribution of feature
i
to the prediction is literally the output of subnetwork
i
. Plot it, and you are looking at the model's own internal arithmetic, not a reconstruction of it.
This article implements the architecture for MetaTrader 5. It trains a five-feature NAM in PyTorch, exports it to ONNX, and runs it in an Expert Advisor that reads both the signal and each feature's exact contribution on every closed bar. Three files ship with this article — an include class wrapping the ONNX model, the EA itself, and the Python training/export script — each walked through with its actual code below, not just described in prose.
Scope note:
this article assumes familiarity with MQL5's ONNX integration (
OnnxCreateFromBuffer
,
OnnxRun
) and with training a small neural network in PyTorch. It does not re-derive backpropagation or ONNX's tensor format from scratch.
There's a practical reason this matters beyond intellectual tidiness. Most ML-driven EAs treat the model as a signal generator and stop there: a number comes out, a trade fires or doesn't, and the "why" lives nowhere except in whatever the training script's loss curve looked like weeks earlier. That's fine until the model starts behaving strangely on a live account, at which point the operator is debugging a black box with nothing but the inputs and the output to go on. A NAM exposes the middle of the chain: it shows how much each input moves the output and in which direction. It does so within the same forward pass, with no separate explainability step.
This does not remove standard caveats. A NAM still depends on feature quality, can overfit on small datasets, and requires out-of-sample checks of its shape functions before live use. What it removes is one specific failure mode: attribution that quietly stops matching the model the moment someone asks for it in detail.
Architecture and Mathematics
A generalized additive model, going back decades before neural networks were practical, predicts an outcome as a sum of one-dimensional functions, one per feature
g(y) = beta + f_1(x_1) + f_2(x_2) + ... + f_K(x_K)
where
g
is a link function (identity here, since the EA treats the output as a continuous directional signal rather than a probability) and each
f_i
is fit independently of the others, seeing only its own feature. Classically these
f_i
were splines or boosted trees. A Neural Additive Model replaces each
f_i
with a small neural network — expressive enough to learn a genuinely non-linear shape, but structurally forbidden from looking at any feature but its own.
That structural restriction is the entire point. A plain dense network with five inputs and one hidden layer will happily learn that RSI matters differently depending on what volume is doing — a real interaction, but one that makes "how much did RSI contribute" an ill-posed question. A NAM cannot learn that interaction even if it exists in the data, because
f_1
(RSI's subnetwork) has no path to ever see the volume feature. The trade-off is capped expressivity in exchange for a contribution that is always well-defined. The
original NAM paper
found this trade-off costs surprisingly little accuracy against boosted trees on tabular data, which is the bet this article makes for a five-feature trading signal too.
Five features feed the model, chosen to span momentum, mean-reversion, volume behavior, execution cost, and session timing — deliberately heterogeneous sources so the per-feature attribution has something genuinely different to say about each one:
x1 (FEATURE_RSI_NORM) = normalized RSI, rescaled from [0,100] to [-1,1]
x2 (FEATURE_ATR_MOMENTUM) = one-bar close change divided by ATR (volatility-normalized momentum)
x3 (FEATURE_VOLUME_ZSCORE) = tick-volume z-score over a rolling 20-bar window
x4 (FEATURE_SPREAD_RANGE) = current spread divided by ATR (execution-cost pressure)
x5 (FEATURE_SESSION_SIN) = sin(2*pi*hour/24) — a cyclical encoding of time-of-day
Each subnetwork
f_i
in this implementation is an ExU (exponential unit) layer followed by a linear output — the design the
NAM paper
itself proposes specifically for jagged, non-stationary inputs, where it reports plain ReLU subnetworks tending to fit oversmoothed shape functions. That finding comes from the paper's own tabular benchmarks, not from an ExU-vs-ReLU ablation run on this five-feature trading dataset, so the choice here is best read as adopting the paper's design motivation rather than a result independently verified on this data. An ExU unit centers its input around a learned bias and scales it by an exponentiated weight before the ReLU
y = ReLU(
exp
(w) * (x - b) )
Exponentiating the weight lets a single parameter reach a much larger effective range early in training than a linear weight would, which in practice produces shape functions with sharper transitions rather than the flat, averaged-out curves plain ReLU units tend to settle on. The trade-off is sensitivity to learning rate — too high, and the exponentiated weight explodes; this is why the training script uses a conservative default of 1e-3 with weight decay.
Five Independent Subnetworks Summed Into One Signal
Fig. 1. The additive architecture: five independent subnetworks, one per feature, summed into a single signal.
One design choice worth defending explicitly: the link function
g
is the identity, not a sigmoid. A sigmoid output would frame the prediction as a directional probability, which is the more common choice for a trading signal, but it comes with a real cost specific to additive models — a sigmoid squashes the sum
after
the additive structure has already done its work, which means two very different combinations of feature contributions can map to the same squashed probability.
That's a small loss of information anywhere, but it is a particularly bad one here, because it would mean the number printed for "ATR momentum contribution" no longer corresponds cleanly to how much that feature moved the final probability — the sigmoid's own curvature gets mixed into the interpretation. Keeping
g
as the identity means the threshold check in the EA (
InpSignalThreshold
) operates directly on the additive sum, and every contribution printed to the chart is on the same linear scale the threshold itself uses.
The choice of exactly five features is also a deliberate constraint rather than an attempt at an exhaustive feature set. Every additional feature is a fully independent subnetwork with its own weights, which means training data requirements grow roughly linearly with feature count — a NAM does not get the same "the model will figure out which features matter" slack a regularized dense network gets, because there's no shared capacity across features to lean on. Five features chosen to be maximally distinct from each other (momentum, mean-reversion, volume, cost, time) was judged a better use of a modest training set than fifteen features with a lot of redundant information between them, several of which would end up with noisy, under-trained shape functions.
It's also worth being precise about what "interpretable" is and isn't claiming here. A NAM tells you, exactly, how much each feature contributed to this prediction. It does not tell you why the feature's shape function looks the way it does, in the sense of a causal market explanation — that's still something a human has to look at the plotted curve and reason about, the same way a statistician reasons about a fitted spline. What the architecture buys is removing one layer of uncertainty (is this attribution even a faithful description of what the model computed?) so the remaining, harder question (does this relationship make economic sense?) can be examined directly against a curve that is guaranteed to be the real one.
NAMEngine.mqh: Feature Engineering and the ONNX Bridge
CNAMEngine
is the whole model wrapper: it owns the feature buffer, loads the ONNX graph, runs the forward pass, and hands back both the signal and the per-feature contributions. Six methods matter; each is walked through with its actual code below.
The feature enum and class declaration
. The ordering here is the single source of truth shared between this file and the Python training script — the ONNX graph was trained with features in exactly this order, and nothing enforces that agreement except both files agreeing to it by hand
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Feature index enumeration                                        |
//| Fixes the ordering of the NAM's additive inputs. The ONNX graph  |
//| was trained with features in exactly this order, so this enum is |
//| the single source of truth shared between the Python training    |
//| script and the EA.                                               |
