This engine deliberately avoids
ONNX_NO_CONVERSION
. Using that flag would require both the input and output matrices to be declared as
matrix<float>
rather than the plain
double
arrays used here, and would need each output matrix pre-sized before the call — two extra failure points for a five-feature model that gains nothing from the tighter type control.
ONNX_DEFAULT
's automatic double-to-float conversion costs a negligible amount of precision on inputs already scaled to roughly [-2, 2].
Run — the forward pass and attribution readout
. The two-output shape is what makes attribution free at inference time. Most ONNX-consuming EAs export a single scalar and stop there; this model's
contributions
output tensor exists purely so the second half of the additive sum is available without a second forward pass or any post-hoc estimation
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CNAMEngine::Run                                                  |
//| Runs one ONNX forward pass over the current feature vector and   |
//| reads back both outputs. Because a NAM's second output is the    |
//| individual f_i(x_i) values by construction, no separate          |
//| explainability pass is needed here — attribution comes for free  |
//| with the prediction.                                             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CNAMEngine::Run(
void
)
  {
if
(!m_is_ready)
return
(
false
);
double
input_matrix[
1
][NAM_FEATURE_COUNT];
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < NAM_FEATURE_COUNT; i++)
      input_matrix[
0
][i] = m_feature_vec[i];
double
signal_out[
1
][
1
];
double
contrib_out[
1
][NAM_FEATURE_COUNT];
if
(!
OnnxRun
(m_onnx_handle,
ONNX_DEFAULT
,input_matrix,signal_out,contrib_out))
     {
PrintFormat
(
"CNAMEngine::Run: OnnxRun failed, error %d"
,
GetLastError
());
return
(
false
);
     }
//--- unpack: signal is the single scalar, contributions map 1:1 to
//--- FEATURE_* indices by the order the model was trained on
m_signal = signal_out[
0
][
0
];
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < NAM_FEATURE_COUNT; i++)
      m_contrib[i] = contrib_out[
0
][i];
return
(
true
);
  }
Contribution and Release
. The two remaining methods are small but both matter:
Contribution()
degrades to 0.0 on a bad index rather than trapping, since it's called from chart-display code;
Release()
is safe to call repeatedly, including from the destructor after an explicit call in
OnDeinit()
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CNAMEngine::Contribution                                         |
//| Returns the last-computed additive contribution for one feature. |
//| Out-of-range indices return 0.0 rather than trapping, since this |
//| is called from chart-display code where a bad index should       |
//| degrade gracefully, not crash the EA.                            |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CNAMEngine::Contribution(
const
int
feature)
const
{
if
(feature <
0
|| feature >= NAM_FEATURE_COUNT)
return
(
0.0
);
return
(m_contrib[feature]);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CNAMEngine::Release                                              |
//| Frees the ONNX handle exactly once. Safe to call multiple times, |
//| including from the destructor after an explicit Release() in     |
//| OnDeinit().                                                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CNAMEngine::Release(
void
)
  {
if
(m_onnx_handle !=
INVALID_HANDLE
)
     {
OnnxRelease
(m_onnx_handle);
      m_onnx_handle =
INVALID_HANDLE
;
     }
   m_is_ready =
false
;
  }
