ExU and FeatureNet — the shape functions.
Its structure mirrors the additive architecture directly: one
FeatureNet
module per input column, never sharing weights, never seeing another column's data. The exponentiated weight in
ExU
is what gives these subnetworks sharper, less-saturated shape functions than plain ReLU would fit
class
ExU(nn.Module):
"""
    Exp-centered unit: y = relu(exp(w) @ (x - b)). The NAM paper found ExU units
    fit sharper, less-saturated shape functions than plain ReLU for the
    kind of jagged, non-smooth relationships financial features tend to
    have, at the cost of needing a smaller learning rate.
    """
def
__init__(self, in_features, out_features):
super
().__init__()
        self.w = nn.Parameter(torch.randn(in_features, out_features) *
0.5
)
        self.b = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(in_features))
def
forward(self, x):
# --- x: (batch, in_features) -> centered, then exp-scaled weights
centered = x - self.b
return
torch.relu(centered @ torch.exp(self.w))
class
FeatureNet(nn.Module):
"""One small subnetwork per feature: this IS the shape function f_i."""
def
__init__(self, hidden=
32
):
super
().__init__()
        self.exu = ExU(
1
, hidden)
        self.out = nn.Linear(hidden,
1
, bias=
False
)
def
forward(self, x):
# --- x: (batch, 1) -> (batch, 1); this scalar-in scalar-out shape
# --- is exactly what makes the output directly plottable as f_i(x_i)
return
self.out(self.exu(x))
